  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 328 AM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nThrough tonight: Early morning surface analysis had a cold front \nnearly along the Natchez Trace. Local radars had a band of showers \nalong and ahead of the cold front. A surface high to the northwest \nwill build into the region and push the cold front southeast of our \nCWA but our winds aloft will become nearly zonal before backing more \nto the southwest later today. The will result in the chance for \nadditional light showers behind the cold front. Patchy dense fog \nwill be possible ahead of the cold front this morning but a Dense \nFog Advisory is not currently anticipated. The chance for rain will \nend by this evening. Otherwise, cooler than normal temperatures are \nexpected today over our delta region with highs in the 50s while \nwarmer than normal temperatures continue across the southeast half \nof the CWA. It will be much cooler across the CWA tonight but a \ndecent amount of cloud cover will continue across the southern half \nof the CWA holding temperatures slightly warmer than normal. Across \nour northern zones temperatures will fall into the 30s by morning \nwhich is cooler than normal. /22/ \n\nTuesday - Sunday\n\nRain chances (30-80%) return late Tuesday through Wednesday as a \nshortwave progress over the area. There is increasing confidence of \nmeasurable rainfall over the event; however; with the ongoing \ndrought conditions, WPC QPF estimates 1/4 in. near NE LA/SE AR to 1 \nin. near eastern MS. High end QPF totals (1 in to 2 in) are also in \nthe forecast, but as previously stated, drought conditions may \nprevent this occurrence. Once the wave passes, cooler temperatures \nwill follow for Thanksgiving and Friday. Temperatures are expected \nto increase through the weekend as a sfc high move over the \nsoutheast CONUS. /SW/",
